<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.5.5: Using Graphical and Algebraic Approaches for Finding Unknowns of Linear Functions.</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00914</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.5.5: Using Graphical and Algebraic Approaches for Finding Unknowns of Linear Functions.</md:title>
    <md:uuid>1e20846a-9843-4d86-b4c7-506abe29692f</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity </title>
<para>The college student who is looking for a data plan for her cell phone found a third option, which also offers a 2-gigabyte monthly allowance for data.</para>
<para>The monthly cost of this option can be represented by function \(C\), defined by \( C(x)=30x+5\), where \(x\) is the gigabytes of data used over the monthly allowance.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="1" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find \(C(0.7)\) and explain what it means in this situation. </item>
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> \(C(0.7)=26\) because \(30(0.7)+5=26\). Here is a possible explanation: It means that the cost would be $26 if she used 0.7 gigabyte over the 2-gigabytes allowance in a month. </para>
  <item>Her budget is still $50 a month. To find out how many gigabytes of data she could use if she chose this plan, she writes \(C(x)=50\) and solves for \(x\). What value of \(x\) makes this equation true? Be prepared to show your reasoning. </item>
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> \(x=1.5\). Here is a possible explanation: Because \( C(x)=30x+5\), we can write \(C(x)=50\) as \(30x+5=50\) and solve for \(x\), which gives 1.5. </para>
</list>
<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title>Press Pause</title>
<para>At this point, students need to be able to evaluate and interpret equations given in function notation. If students continue to struggle, select examples of cool-downs from this unit to highlight and clarify misconceptions, and then have students work to revise cool-downs and reflect on their misconceptions. Practice problems 1, 3, 4, and 5 from this lesson all provide opportunities for practice and additional formative assessment.</para></section>
</content>
